<h2>Four examples of evidence-led module triage</h2>
<h3>Case 1 — Several modules disappear after a weak-battery event</h3>
<p>A car presents communication faults immediately after repeated starting attempts. The first decision is whether the diagnostic interface and host still meet their own requirements, and whether the vehicle electrical state plausibly interferes with module responses. Record battery condition and original errors under an approved, safe procedure. If the scanner sees powertrain data but not several body units, the gateway or network reachability matters. Do not replace all the reportedly missing modules or clear the history simply because a fault list names them. The correct next source is the <a href="/guides/bmw-no-communication-with-obd-scanner/">no-communication guide</a>, followed by the module-specific service information.</p>
<h3>Case 2 — VANOS code after maintenance, engine still runs</h3>
<p>One stored camshaft timing code can appear to implicate a VANOS solenoid. The meaningful distinction is whether commanded and measured cam angle differ under the specified operating condition and whether the diagnostic software exposes the relevant data. Also preserve related faults and maintenance context. The exact engine family changes the interpretation, so an assumed part failure is premature. A tool that only reads generic codes may not be enough for further evidence. The <a href="/guides/bmw-vanos-diagnostic-tool/">VANOS guide</a> explains the data and tool class needed without promising a one-code fix.</p>
<h3>Case 3 — Parking brake service requested on an unfamiliar BMW</h3>
<p>A tablet may advertise a universal electronic parking brake menu. Before using it, identify whether the actual car has that brake architecture, whether the software's exact chassis coverage includes the requested service mode, and whether the procedure is safe in its present workshop condition. Braking work has a higher risk than reading faults. If any precondition is undocumented, stop rather than trying menu items experimentally. The specialist <a href="/guides/bmw-electric-parking-brake-service-mode-scanner/">EMF article</a> remains independent for those distinctions and must not be replaced by a generic reset summary.</p>
<h3>Case 4 — Recurrent battery warning with no single clear code</h3>
<p>The car intermittently warns about a battery but the scanner reports limited fault information. Battery charge acceptance, sleep patterns, parasitic draw, Intelligent Battery Sensor messages and electrical measurement are different evidence types. An energy-management log may hint at wake events without directly measuring the source of current draw. Avoid disconnecting components at random and changing sleep behavior during a test; that can invalidate measurements. Begin with the <a href="/guides/bmw-battery-drain-diagnostic-tool/">battery-drain research</a> and the applicable manufacturer procedure, not a battery-registration button selected as a guess.</p>